DAY 48 - PART A: THEORY
Autoscaling: Pods (HPA) and Nodes (Cluster Autoscaler / Karpenter)
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THREE LAYERS OF AUTOSCALING
------------------------------
  HPA  Horizontal Pod Autoscaler   more/fewer pod replicas
  VPA  Vertical Pod Autoscaler     bigger/smaller requests per pod
  Node autoscaling                 more/fewer nodes
       Cluster Autoscaler (CA), Karpenter, EKS Auto Mode,
       GKE/AKS built-in autoscaler

They work together:
  load up -> HPA adds pods -> pods Pending (no room) ->
  node autoscaler adds nodes -> pods scheduled
  load down -> HPA removes pods -> nodes under-used ->
  node autoscaler drains and removes nodes


2. HPA
------
  apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
  kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
  metadata:
    name: web
  spec:
    scaleTargetRef:
      apiVersion: apps/v1
      kind: Deployment
      name: web
    minReplicas: 2
    maxReplicas: 10
    metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 50     # % of the pods' CPU REQUEST

desired = ceil(current replicas x current usage / target)
Needs metrics-server and CPU REQUESTS on the pods (no request = no %).
Scale-down waits 5 minutes by default (stabilization window) to avoid
flapping. Custom/external metrics (queue length, RPS) via Prometheus
Adapter or KEDA (Week 9).


3. CLUSTER AUTOSCALER
---------------------
Watches for Pending pods that would fit on a new node of some node
group -> increases that group's desired size (ASG). Removes nodes that
are under-used and whose pods can move elsewhere (respecting PDBs).
Works with node groups of fixed instance types.

KARPENTER
  Looks at Pending pods and launches EC2 instances of the best size
  and type directly (no node groups), consolidates aggressively, great
  with Spot. Popular on EKS. EKS Auto Mode is built on it.


4. SPOT / PREEMPTIBLE NODES
---------------------------
60-90% cheaper, can be taken back with 2 minutes' notice. Fine for
stateless, replicated, interruption-tolerant workloads; use PDBs,
multiple instance types, and keep critical pods on on-demand nodes
(taints/affinity, Week 5).


5. GOTCHAS
----------
- No requests -> HPA can't compute; CA can't decide node fit.
- Requests too high -> waste; too low -> noisy neighbours.
- maxReplicas x request must fit within max nodes.
- Scale-up of nodes takes minutes; keep some headroom for spikes.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- HPA scales pods on % of request; CA/Karpenter scale nodes on
  Pending pods.
- Requests are the input to all autoscalers.
- Spot saves money for tolerant workloads.
